WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.459
Today's video is going to be about my experiences during undergrad studying mathematics.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:13.439
Now I did do a double major in physics and maths and I've already made a video called my regrets studying physics

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:18.119
so I thought it would only be fair to make this one. To start off

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:22.349
I'll tell you some things that I definitely don't regret about studying maths.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:26.309
Number one would be that I don't regret the time that I spent studying

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:32.759
so I don't regret the time that I spent studying and learning mathematical ideas instead of

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:38.430
anything else that I could have been doing whether it's going out and partying or doing any other activity.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:42.450
I don't regret that I spent a lot of that time studying.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:47.999
I also don't regret any of the people that I've met whilst I've been pursuing maths

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:52.558
whether it was like lecturers or students in my class and my peers

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.969
I've really enjoyed some of the friendships I've made through math classes.

00:00:56.969 --> 00:01:00.299
And in fact, some of my best friends have come from there.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:08.010
So let's talk about some of the things that I do regret and this is actually pretty nuanced because I would say

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:15.239
overall, I'm glad I majored in mathematics. I think it's a good major and I definitely got a lot out of it

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:21.779
this is just inviting over Captain Hindsight to see a few things that I guess I

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.119
feel not the greatest about. So the first would be thinking that I was good at math

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:32.339
just because I've done a course on it or because I could get a good grade in that course.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.670
I also had a lot of confusion between

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:44.070
getting a good grade on something and that meaning that I actually enjoyed it. What I mean is that I sort of felt like

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.649
sometimes more than I enjoyed maths

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:48.649
I just enjoyed the

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.389
satisfaction of getting a good grade on something or

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.100
the feeling that I could get from doing maths that had nothing to do with maths at all.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:03.869
And that was more about my personal craving to do something that satisfied me and to get, you know, instant

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:06.959
satisfaction in a way that doing like a maths assignment,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.869
especially the low-hanging fruit or the easy problems in math, can give you. You know, if you can apply some of these Ideas that you've learned,

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:19.359
do the homework problem, hand it in and get feedback pretty quickly afterwards,

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.720
it kind of feels good and it feels like you're accomplishing something.

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:28.390
It feels good to just to be able to finish something. That's what I'm especially talking about

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.879
these sort of low hanging fruit problems in math

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.420
they really gave me the sense of satisfaction and

00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:42.399
I guess I enjoyed that. I enjoyed feeling like I was good at something and I think it was good for my self confidence

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:49.479
but I think I confused that feeling I got from math with actually enjoying it because

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:56.170
sometimes I didn't enjoy it at all but it was just masked by this feeling that oh, well, I got a good grade

00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:02.979
so I must be really enjoying and I must be passionate about this subject when sometimes I wasn't. Don't get me wrong

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.069
there are some areas of math I really do enjoy

00:03:06.409 --> 00:03:10.659
for example, I found my dynamical systems course really interesting

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:14.440
but then there were some courses that I took, I think for other reasons.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.060
Maybe one of those other reasons was to stroke my own ego,

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:23.049
like I would take courses that I thought were notoriously difficult

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:27.669
just because I felt like I should take that challenge and that

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:28.760
people

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:35.109
taking those courses will seem to be the best at math and maybe it was the perception that I wanted other people to believe

00:03:35.109 --> 00:03:36.430
I was good at maths as well.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:38.000
So I feel like

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.139
maybe not all the choices I made in terms of which classes I picked and everything like that, where I put my effort, was

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.160
made based on what I actually enjoyed but it was based on other things.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.829
It was based on what I thought I was good at from these

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:58.629
arbitrary grades and measures and it was based on what I thought was perceived as like the best math to be doing or the most

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:04.268
prestigious math to be doing when really none of those things mattered as much. So at the time

00:04:04.269 --> 00:04:07.569
I wish I guess I'd been a little bit more aware of what I actually

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:13.720
enjoyed and what I didn't. My second regret is probably thinking that some of the math was more

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:19.779
abstract than it really was. I was presented with a lot of these really abstract ideas in math and

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:22.450
one thing about them is that they're very hard

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:28.869
I guess to show to people who are outside of mathematics completely, and have them understand what's going on.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:35.739
So it kind of creates this feeling of like an elite little club of people who are in this math class, and they

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:42.760
understand something that kind of very few other people understand and it's kind of a big barrier to entry as well

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:45.050
sometimes when it comes to math.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:51.639
So there were a lot of these abstract ideas and I guess I didn't ever really put much effort into trying to

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:56.739
communicate them to other people and I would often maybe brush them off as just being

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:02.499
crazily complex math ideas that you'd never understand unless you're in this class.

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:06.820
But it also affected my own thinking about these ideas

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:13.029
I guess sometimes I felt like certain exercises and problems that I was working on only

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:18.669
existed in this strange realm which consisted of the lecturer's own mind,

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:25.510
my notes and then on the exam and the problems didn't seem to exist anywhere outside of this. And

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.750
that affected the way that I thought about them.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:29.750
I found them

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:32.290
not so interesting or important and I only

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:36.640
studied for them in a way that I was good at answering them on the exam but

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:39.790
didn't have much knowledge about these problems to apply

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:46.450
outside of that context. That lack of understanding of the bigger picture of some of these math ideas is

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:53.499
one of my regrets and actually I realized it from an unlikely source, and it was from a Vsauce video.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:55.899
I think the video I remember it's called like

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.049
the  brachistochrone and it's

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:01.220
actually about

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.749
finding the quickest way that you can fall between two points

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:10.749
but the fact is that vsauce made this really cool video about this idea and linking it to all these other physical

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:11.710
ideas

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:18.370
and it was really cool video and I was thinking, like I have actually done lots of problems before on the subject this

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:26.380
brachistochrone in math class and physics class and I never actually realized it was cool. I never thought about it as being

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:32.440
interesting in this context for other people and I kind of realized that a lot of the ideas

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:36.980
I've studied were actually interesting outside the context of the exam

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:41.539
But I've never been able to see that before. I've since seen a lot of other videos

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:46.459
someone like 3Blue1Brown presents math ideas, and I watch them and I think

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:48.979
Wow, this is actually interesting

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:55.249
these ideas are actually interesting, these ideas that I have learned about at uni but never cared about .

00:06:55.710 --> 00:07:00.680
I wished that I'd seen a lot of these deeper meanings at the time and actually

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.830
used them to be more engaged with what I was learning.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:09.559
Again, sure, I did appreciate some things. I mean I managed to get through the math degree

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:14.179
so I had some amount of inspiration behind me and what I'm doing now on YouTube

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:15.620
I think you can see that

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:23.390
I do have a passion for math somewhere down there, but it's definitely been very small at times and has come and gone. But I

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:29.329
really feel like it wasn't until after I finished a lot of these courses that I realized

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:33.529
these ideas were actually cool and that I probably should have spent more time

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:40.939
trying to understand how I could communicate them to other people because it would have helped me appreciate that they were cool.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:45.200
So those are my thoughts here. It's just based on my personal experience

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:51.409
so let me know if you can relate to any of these ideas. And as always thanks for watching [Vsauce reference]

